  "facts": {
    "value": "The resident lives in a one-bedroom ground floor flat and pays a variable service charge. She told us she has difficulty with her memory. She raised concerns that the service charges were unclear, some services were not being provided, and the landlord did not properly monitor their quality or frequency. On 4 March 2023, the resident queried the accuracy of the service charges for the TV aerial and door entry system, saying these services were not provided to the property. On 9 May 2023, the resident raised a complaint because she had not had a response to her query. On 6 June 2023, the landlord issued its stage 1 response, confirming the charges for the TV aerial and door entry system had been removed and apologising for its error and delay, offering £100 compensation. On 14 June 2023, the resident escalated her complaint because she had not received a breakdown of her service charges that she had requested in 2021, felt wrongly charged for a fire safety callout caused by a contractor, and wanted clarity on her refund and a 4-year audit. On 12 October 2023, the landlord issued its stage 2 response, confirming the previous 4 years of service charges were applied correctly with no evidence of contractor fault, and awarded £100 compensation for delay. The resident told us she still had concerns because she thought some service charges had been introduced without consultation, was unsure if she had been refunded for incorrect charges, the landlord had not explained how it monitors the quality of services provided, communication about service charges had been poor for 4 years, and service charge information was inaccessible and poorly formatted.",
